#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fleet-local-check — health checks Gatus (or any remote prober) structurally can't do.

Some things aren't reachable from a central health page: a service with no listening port, a
local network mount, "is my monitor itself up?", or a check that needs local DB/CLI access. Run
this ON the node that can see them. It reads a config of typed checks, and emails (via fleet-mail)
when a check TRANSITIONS (ok→fail or fail→ok), and when a check is failing on its FIRST run (a new
check, or a lost state file) — a state file dedups, so a tight cadence never spams. State is saved
only AFTER the email is sent: if sending fails, the next run retries instead of forgetting the alert.

THREE STATES, NOT TWO (guide § 17, "A probe that could not answer has not told you anything").
A check returns OK (verified good), FAIL (verified bad) or UNKNOWN (the probe itself could not
answer: a timeout, an unreadable table). A probe that timed out has NOT told you the thing is
broken, and scoring it as a failure is how a monitor cries wolf on exactly the busy nights you can
least afford it. So:
  · UNKNOWN carries the previous state forward — no transition, no mail;
  · but it is COUNTED: after UNKNOWN_FAIL_STREAK consecutive unknowns the check FAILS, worded as
    what it is ("could not determine state for N consecutive runs"), because a monitor that has
    been unable to see for an hour is itself an outage. Silence must not read as health.

Config (default $HOME/.config/fleet-monitoring/local-checks.conf, override --config/$FLEET_LOCAL_CHECKS):
    type | name | arg …
      service | <name> | <launchd-label | systemd-unit>     is that service running?
      mount   | <name> | <path>                             is <path> a mount? (mount point + table)
      http    | <name> | <url>                              does <url> return 200?
      command | <name> | <shell command>                    exit 0 = OK · exit 3 = UNKNOWN · else FAIL
      hash    | <name> | <[host:]installed file> | <reference>  byte-identical to its reference copy?
      synclag | <name> | <[host:]git dir> | <max hours> [| <job>]  fetched within <max hours>? (and the sync
                                                                job's last run succeeded, if <job> is named)
      prompts | <name> | local | <ssh-host>             (macOS) a privacy prompt waiting unanswered on that
                                                        Mac's screen for PROMPT_WAIT_MIN minutes?
    Two directives change how a named check ALERTS, not what it checks:
      solo    | <name>      it mails on its OWN (an alert you want as its own message). It stays in the
                            combined digest's body, but no longer triggers the digest — one event, one mail.
      away    | <name>      its target may simply be away (a laptop asleep or travelling): its UNKNOWNs carry
                            the last state forward and never escalate to a failure.
A malformed line, or a directive naming no check, is REPORTED as a failing check, never silently skipped.
A check may return SEVERAL results (one per row of a registry it reads); each is its own row, with its
own state and transitions.

IMPORTANT (the `mount` type never reads INSIDE the share): on macOS a scheduled job's first read inside
a share can raise a privacy prompt that waits until someone answers it — on an unattended Mac, forever
(platforms/macos.md). So `mount` checks the mount point and the mount table (ismount +
`mount`/`findmnt`) instead. ⚠ That is less I/O, not none: ismount() stats the mount point (a server
round-trip on a network mount; an unreachable server reads as "not a mount point"), and the table
read can block on an unresponsive server. So the probe retries, and a table it cannot read is
UNKNOWN, never "unmounted". It catches "unmounted", not "mounted-but-hung" — for hung-detection, use a
`command` check that reads inside the share (30 s limit; a timeout is UNKNOWN), run by a program macOS
has approved.

WHY `hash` AND `synclag` EXIST. A config manager keeps the nodes it manages in step — and nothing
else. A file you installed by hand (on a node it doesn't manage, or outside its tree) falls behind
the repo silently: the fix reaches every other node and the check that would notice is the one you
never wrote. `hash` compares the installed copy with its reference. On a node the config manager
doesn't manage, the installed file and the repo copy are on DIFFERENT machines, so the installed side
may be `host:/path`: it is read over SSH from the node that holds the repo. An SSH failure is UNKNOWN,
never a pass. `synclag` asks whether sync is
actually HAPPENING: a status command that never fetches reports "clean" while days behind (guide
05b), so it reads the age of the last successful fetch instead.

WHY `prompts` EXISTS (guide 17c). macOS asks before a program it has not approved reads a protected place
(a network share, iCloud Drive …), and the read WAITS until someone answers. On an unattended Mac nobody
does: the job hangs, and every package update makes a new program that is asked again. macOS's privacy
service logs each request and its answer within milliseconds, so a request still unanswered after
PROMPT_WAIT_MIN means a prompt is on the screen. Only the per-user service's requests are tracked (the
system-wide one logs some that are never answered at all), a log with no requests and no replies is
UNKNOWN (silence is what a broken query also prints), and the log is read with /usr/bin/log — in zsh a
bare `log` is a built-in. A laptop that may be away: mark the row `away`. --dry-run never advances the
scan position it keeps in $FLEET_PROMPT_STATE.

Exit: 0 all ok · 1 one or more failing · 3 an email failed to send (the checks it covered keep their
previous state, so the next run sends it again; every other check's state is saved).
Flags: --dry-run (print, no email/state), --always (email regardless).
Environment: $FLEET_MAILER (default ~/.local/bin/fleet-mail), $FLEET_LOCAL_CHECK_STATE (state file).
"""
import argparse, datetime, hashlib, json, os, platform, re, shlex, shutil, subprocess, sys, time

STATE = os.environ.get("FLEET_LOCAL_CHECK_STATE",
                       os.path.expanduser("~/.config/fleet-monitoring/local-check.state"))
DEFAULT_CONFIG = os.path.expanduser("~/.config/fleet-monitoring/local-checks.conf")
MAILER = os.environ.get("FLEET_MAILER", os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin/fleet-mail"))
IS_MAC = platform.system() == "Darwin"
UNKNOWN_FAIL_STREAK = 4    # at a 15-min cadence: an hour blind before blindness itself alerts
MOUNT_RETRIES = 3          # transient slowness must not look like an unmount
MOUNT_TIMEOUT = 20         # reading the mount table can block behind a busy network server
RETRY_PAUSE = 2            # seconds between mount attempts
DRY_RUN = False            # set by main(): a check that keeps its own state must not advance it on a dry run

def sh(args, timeout=15, shell=False):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, shell=shell)
        return p.returncode, (p.stdout or "").strip(), (p.stderr or "").strip()
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "", "timed out"
    except Exception as e:
        return 1, "", str(e)

# Each check returns (name, state, detail) with state True / False / None (= UNKNOWN).

def chk_service(name, label):
    if IS_MAC:
        rc, out, err = sh(["launchctl", "print", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{label}"])
        if rc != 0 and not out:
            # "not loaded" prints output with a non-zero rc; NO output means launchctl itself
            # could not answer — the probe failed, not the service.
            if rc == 124 or not err or "could not find" not in err.lower():
                return name, None, f"launchctl gave no answer ({err or 'rc=' + str(rc)})"
            return name, False, "NOT loaded"
        ok = "state = running" in out
    else:
        rc, out, err = sh(["systemctl", "is-active", label])
        if rc == 124 or not out:
            return name, None, f"systemctl gave no answer ({err or 'rc=' + str(rc)})"
        ok = out.strip() == "active"
    return name, ok, ("running" if ok else "NOT running")

ISMOUNT = "import os, sys; sys.exit(0 if os.path.ismount(sys.argv[1]) else 1)"

def _ismount(path):
    """True / False, or None when the stat did not answer in time. It runs in a CHILD process with a time
    limit: os.path.ismount() stats the mount point, and on a dead network server that stat can block
    for as long as the server is gone — inside this process it would freeze every check after it."""
    rc, _, _ = sh([sys.executable, "-c", ISMOUNT, path], timeout=MOUNT_TIMEOUT)
    return True if rc == 0 else (False if rc == 1 else None)

def chk_mount(name, path):
    last = ""
    for attempt in range(MOUNT_RETRIES):
        mounted = _ismount(path)
        if mounted is None:
            return name, None, f"the mount point did not answer within {MOUNT_TIMEOUT} s — not an unmount"
        if not mounted:
            last = "NOT mounted"
        else:
            tbl = "mount" if shutil.which("mount") else "findmnt"
            rc, out, err = sh([tbl] + ([] if tbl == "mount" else [path]), timeout=MOUNT_TIMEOUT)
            if rc != 0 or not out:
                # The PROBE failed, not the mount. Never report this as an unmount.
                return name, None, f"could not read the mount table ({err or 'rc=' + str(rc)})"
            if any(path in l for l in out.splitlines()):
                return name, True, "mounted"
            last = "mountpoint present but not in the mount table"
        if attempt < MOUNT_RETRIES - 1:
            time.sleep(RETRY_PAUSE)
    return name, False, f"{last} (confirmed over {MOUNT_RETRIES} attempts)"

def chk_http(name, url):
    rc, out, err = sh(["curl", "-s", "-o", "/dev/null", "-w", "%{http_code}", "-m", "10", url], timeout=15)
    if rc in (28, 124):                 # curl timed out, or we killed it: no answer, not a "no"
        return name, None, "no response within 10 s"
    if rc != 0:                         # refused, unresolvable, TLS failure: a definite answer
        return name, False, f"request failed (curl exit {rc})"
    return name, out.strip() == "200", f"HTTP {out.strip()}"

def chk_command(name, cmd):
    rc, _, err = sh(cmd, timeout=30, shell=True)
    if rc == 0:
        return name, True, "ok"
    if rc in (3, 124):                  # exit 3 = UNKNOWN (the Nagios convention); 124 = timed out
        return name, None, f"could not determine (exit {rc}){(' — ' + err) if err else ''}"
    return name, False, f"exit {rc}{(' — ' + err) if err else ''}"

def _sha256(path):
    with open(path, "rb") as f:
        return hashlib.sha256(f.read()).hexdigest()

# `host:/path` or `host:~/path`. A one-letter "host" is a drive letter (C:/…), so it stays local.
REMOTE_PATH = re.compile(r"^([^\s/:~]{2,}):([/~].*)$")

def _sha256_remote(host, path):
    """(sha, None) · (None, "MISSING") · (None, "<why it could not answer>")."""
    q = ('"$HOME"/' + shlex.quote(path[2:])) if path.startswith("~/") else shlex.quote(path)
    script = (f"if [ ! -e {q} ]; then echo '#MISSING'; "
              f"elif h=$( (sha256sum {q} || shasum -a 256 {q}) 2>/dev/null ); then echo \"#SHA ${{h%% *}}\"; "
              f"else echo '#UNREADABLE'; fi")
    rc, out, err = sh(["ssh", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=8", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", host, script], timeout=25)
    line = next((l for l in out.splitlines() if l.startswith("#")), "")
    if rc == 0 and line.startswith("#SHA ") and len(line) > 5:
        return line[5:].strip(), None
    if rc == 0 and line == "#MISSING":
        return None, "MISSING"
    if rc == 0 and line == "#UNREADABLE":
        return None, f"cannot read it on {host}"
    return None, f"could not reach {host} ({(err or f'rc={rc}').strip()[:60]})"

def chk_hash(name, arg):
    installed, _, reference = (p.strip() for p in arg.partition("|"))
    try:
        want = _sha256(os.path.expanduser(reference))
    except OSError:
        return name, False, f"reference copy missing ({reference}) — not silently skipped"
    remote = REMOTE_PATH.match(installed)
    if remote:
        got, why = _sha256_remote(*remote.groups())
        if why == "MISSING":
            return name, False, f"{installed} is NOT installed"
        if why:
            return name, None, f"{why} — whether {installed} is current is unknown"
        return name, got == want, ("matches its reference" if got == want
                                   else f"{installed} differs from {reference} — reinstall it")
    try:
        got = _sha256(os.path.expanduser(installed))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return name, False, f"{installed} is NOT installed"
    except OSError as e:
        return name, None, f"cannot read {installed} ({e})"
    return name, got == want, ("matches its reference" if got == want
                               else f"{installed} differs from {reference} — reinstall it")

def _launchd_last_run(job, out):
    """Parse `launchctl print`'s output for a job's last run (see _job_last_run)."""
    m = re.search(r"^\s*last exit code = (.+)$", out, re.M)
    if not m:
        return None, f"launchctl printed no last exit code for {job}"
    code = m.group(1).strip()
    if not code.lstrip("-").isdigit():
        return True, f"sync job {job} has not finished a run yet"
    return int(code) == 0, f"sync job {job} last exited {code}"

def _job_last_run(job):
    """(True|False|None, detail) for a scheduled job's LAST run: a launchd label on macOS, a
    systemd unit elsewhere. False = it failed, or isn't loaded; None = the probe couldn't tell."""
    if IS_MAC:
        rc, out, err = sh(["launchctl", "print", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{job}"])
        if rc != 0 and not out:
            if rc == 124 or not err or "could not find" not in err.lower():
                return None, f"launchctl gave no answer ({err or 'rc=' + str(rc)})"
            return False, f"sync job {job} is NOT loaded"
        return _launchd_last_run(job, out)
    rc, out, err = sh(["systemctl", "show", "-p", "LoadState", "-p", "Result", "-p", "ExecMainStatus", job])
    if rc == 124 or not out:
        return None, f"systemctl gave no answer ({err or 'rc=' + str(rc)})"
    return _systemd_last_run(job, out)

def _systemd_last_run(job, out):
    """Parse `systemctl show`'s properties for a job's last run (see _job_last_run)."""
    props = dict(l.split("=", 1) for l in out.splitlines() if "=" in l)
    # ⚠ An unknown unit reports Result=success, ExecMainStatus=0 — so a misspelled unit would read
    # as a healthy sync. Check it is loaded FIRST (measured on systemd 257).
    if props.get("LoadState") != "loaded":
        return False, f"sync job {job} is NOT loaded ({props.get('LoadState', '?')})"
    # Trust systemd's own verdict (Result). It already honours the unit's SuccessExitStatus=, which a
    # raw exit-code comparison would not; ExecMainStatus is reported, not judged.
    status, result = props.get("ExecMainStatus", "?"), props.get("Result", "")
    if result and result != "success":
        return False, f"sync job {job} last run: {result} (exit {status})"
    return True, f"sync job {job} last run succeeded"

def _fetch_verdict(age, limit, where):
    if age is None:
        return None, f"cannot read {where} (never fetched, or not a git clone)"
    if age > limit:
        return False, (f"last successful fetch {age:.1f} h ago (limit {limit:g} h) — "
                       "a status command that never fetches cannot see this")
    return True, f"fetched {age:.1f} h ago"

def _synclag_remote(host, gitdir, limit, job):
    """(fetched, ran) for a clone on ANOTHER node, read over SSH: the age of its FETCH_HEAD by that
    node's own clock, and (if named) its sync job's last run — launchd or systemd, whichever it has.
    A node that cannot be reached is UNKNOWN, never a failure (mark a laptop `away` too)."""
    q = ('"$HOME"/' + shlex.quote(gitdir[2:])) if gitdir.startswith("~/") else shlex.quote(gitdir)
    f = f"{q}/.git/FETCH_HEAD"
    script = (f"m=$(stat -c %Y {f} 2>/dev/null || stat -f %m {f} 2>/dev/null); "
              f'echo "#MTIME $m"; echo "#NOW $(date +%s)"')
    if job:
        j = shlex.quote(job)
        script += (f"; if command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo '#SYSTEMD'; "
                   f"systemctl show -p LoadState -p Result -p ExecMainStatus {j}; "
                   f"else echo '#LAUNCHD'; launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/{j} 2>&1; fi")
    rc, out, err = sh(["ssh", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=8", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", host, script], timeout=25)
    marks = dict(l.split(" ", 1) for l in out.splitlines() if l.startswith("#") and " " in l)
    if rc == 255 or "#NOW" not in marks:
        unknown = (None, f"could not reach {host} ({(err or f'rc={rc}').strip()[:60]})")
        return unknown, (True, "")
    try:
        age = (float(marks["#NOW"]) - float(marks.get("#MTIME", "").strip())) / 3600.0
    except ValueError:
        age = None
    fetched = _fetch_verdict(age, limit, f"{host}:{gitdir}/.git/FETCH_HEAD")
    if not job:
        return fetched, (True, "")
    body = out.split("#SYSTEMD", 1)[1] if "#SYSTEMD" in out else out.split("#LAUNCHD", 1)[-1]
    if "#SYSTEMD" in out:
        ran = (_systemd_last_run(job, body) if body.strip()
               else (None, f"systemctl on {host} gave no answer"))
    elif "could not find" in body.lower():
        ran = (False, f"sync job {job} is NOT loaded on {host}")
    else:
        ran = _launchd_last_run(job, body)
    return fetched, ran

def chk_synclag(name, arg):
    gitdir, _, rest = (p.strip() for p in arg.partition("|"))
    hours, _, job = (p.strip() for p in rest.partition("|"))
    try:
        limit = float(hours)
    except ValueError:
        return name, False, f"max hours {hours!r} is not a number"
    remote = REMOTE_PATH.match(gitdir)
    if remote:
        fetched, ran = _synclag_remote(remote.group(1), remote.group(2), limit, job)
    else:
        fetch_head = os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(gitdir), ".git", "FETCH_HEAD")
        try:
            age = (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(fetch_head)) / 3600.0
        except OSError:
            age = None
        fetched = _fetch_verdict(age, limit, fetch_head)
        # A fresh fetch is not an applied sync: a job that fetches fine and then fails to apply leaves
        # every fetch-based check green. Naming the job adds its last run's result.
        ran = _job_last_run(job) if job else (True, "")
    for state in (False, None):
        bad = [d for s, d in (fetched, ran) if s is state]
        if bad:
            return name, state, "; ".join(bad) + (" — a fresh fetch does not mean it applied"
                                                   if ran[0] is False and fetched[0] is True else "")
    return name, True, "; ".join(d for _, d in (fetched, ran) if d)

PROMPT_WAIT_MIN = 5
PROMPT_STATE = os.environ.get("FLEET_PROMPT_STATE",
                              os.path.expanduser("~/.config/fleet-monitoring/permission-prompts.state"))
PROMPT_FIRST_SCAN_H = 2     # how far back the very first scan reads
PROMPT_PREDICATE = ('process == "tccd" AND (eventMessage BEGINSWITH "REQUEST:" OR eventMessage BEGINSWITH '
                    '"REPLY:" OR eventMessage BEGINSWITH "AUTHREQ_CTX" OR eventMessage BEGINSWITH '
                    '"AUTHREQ_SUBJECT" OR eventMessage BEGINSWITH "AUTHREQ_PROMPTING" OR eventMessage '
                    'BEGINSWITH "AUTHREQ_RESULT")')
_TCC_LINE = re.compile(r"^(\d{4}-\d\d-\d\d \d\d:\d\d:\d\d)\S*\s+\S+\s+tccd\[(\d+):[^\]]*\]\s+"
                       r"(?:\[[^\]]*\]\s+)?(REQUEST|REPLY|AUTHREQ_CTX|AUTHREQ_SUBJECT|AUTHREQ_PROMPTING|AUTHREQ_RESULT):?\s*(.*)$")

def _prompt_subject(s):
    """A subject path or bundle id -> a short, recognisable name."""
    s = s.strip().rstrip(",").replace("Sub:{", "").rstrip("}")
    m = re.match(r"/opt/homebrew/Cellar/([^/]+)/([^/]+)/", s)
    if m:
        return f"{os.path.basename(s.split(',')[0])} (Homebrew {m.group(1)} {m.group(2)})"
    return os.path.basename(s) if s.startswith("/") else s

def prompt_scan(text, open_reqs, tccd_pids):
    """Fold one window of privacy-service log lines into the open-request map. Returns (open_reqs,
    requests, replies) — the counts are the canary: a log with no traffic at all is not "no prompts"."""
    requests = replies = 0
    for line in text.splitlines():
        m = _TCC_LINE.match(line)
        if not m:
            continue
        when, pid, kind, rest = m.groups()
        mid = re.search(r"msgID=([\d.]+)", rest)
        if not mid:
            continue
        key = f"{pid}:{mid.group(1)}"
        if kind == "REQUEST":
            requests += 1
            fn = re.search(r"function=(\w+)", rest)
            # only the PER-USER service: the system-wide one (tccd_uid=0) logs some requests that are
            # never answered at all, which would be a false alarm every run
            if (fn and fn.group(1).startswith("TCCAccessRequest") and "tccd_uid=0," not in rest
                    and key not in open_reqs):
                open_reqs[key] = {"asked": when, "fn": fn.group(1)}
        elif kind in ("REPLY", "AUTHREQ_RESULT"):
            replies += 1
            open_reqs.pop(key, None)
        else:
            if kind == "AUTHREQ_PROMPTING" and key not in open_reqs:
                open_reqs[key] = {"asked": when, "fn": "TCCAccessRequest"}  # a prompt, on either service
            if key not in open_reqs:
                continue
            r = open_reqs[key]
            svc = re.search(r"service=kTCCService(\w+)", rest)
            if kind in ("AUTHREQ_CTX", "AUTHREQ_PROMPTING") and svc:
                r["service"] = svc.group(1)
            if kind == "AUTHREQ_SUBJECT":
                r["subject"] = _prompt_subject(rest.split("subject=", 1)[-1].split(",")[0])
            elif kind == "AUTHREQ_PROMPTING":
                r["prompted"] = True
                sub = re.search(r"subject=(Sub:\{[^}]*\}?)", rest)
                if sub:
                    r["subject"] = _prompt_subject(sub.group(1))
    # A privacy service that has restarted never replies to its old requests.
    for k in [k for k in open_reqs if k.split(":")[0] not in tccd_pids]:
        open_reqs.pop(k)
    return open_reqs, requests, replies

def prompt_waiting(open_reqs, now):
    """The open requests older than PROMPT_WAIT_MIN, as readable lines (oldest first)."""
    out = []
    for r in sorted(open_reqs.values(), key=lambda r: r["asked"]):
        asked = datetime.datetime.strptime(r["asked"], "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
        mins = (now - asked).total_seconds() / 60
        if mins < PROMPT_WAIT_MIN:
            continue
        who = r.get("subject") or ("an indirect request (e.g. iCloud Drive)" if r["fn"].endswith("Indirect")
                                   else "an unnamed program")
        out.append(f"{who} asks for {r.get('service', 'a permission')} — waiting since {asked:%H:%M} ({int(mins)} min)")
    return out

def chk_prompts(name, arg, now=None):
    host = None if arg.strip() == "local" else arg.strip()
    if host is None and not IS_MAC:
        return name, False, "`prompts` reads macOS's privacy log — this node is not macOS (name a Mac's ssh host)"
    now = now or datetime.datetime.now()
    try:
        with open(PROMPT_STATE) as f:
            state = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        state = {}
    mine = state.setdefault(name, {})
    since = mine.get("scanned")
    start = (datetime.datetime.strptime(since, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S") - datetime.timedelta(minutes=1)
             if since else now - datetime.timedelta(hours=PROMPT_FIRST_SCAN_H))
    cmd = (f"/usr/bin/log show --style compact --start {shlex.quote(start.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'))} "
           f"--predicate {shlex.quote(PROMPT_PREDICATE)} 2>/dev/null; echo '#TCCD'; pgrep -x tccd")
    if host:
        rc, out, err = sh(["ssh", "-o", "ConnectTimeout=8", "-o", "BatchMode=yes", host, cmd], timeout=120)
        if rc == 255 or (rc != 0 and not out):
            return name, None, f"{host} not reachable — not checked (mark it `away` if it is a laptop)"
    else:
        rc, out, err = sh(["/bin/sh", "-c", cmd], timeout=120)
    where = host or "this Mac"
    if "#TCCD" not in out:
        return name, None, f"could not read {where}'s privacy log ({err or 'no output'})"
    text, pids = out.split("#TCCD", 1)
    open_reqs, requests, replies = prompt_scan(text, mine.get("open", {}), set(pids.split()))
    mine.update(open=open_reqs, scanned=now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"))
    if not DRY_RUN:                       # a dry run must not advance the scan position
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(PROMPT_STATE), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = PROMPT_STATE + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as f:
            json.dump(state, f)
        os.replace(tmp, PROMPT_STATE)
    if requests == 0 or replies == 0:
        return name, None, (f"{where}'s privacy log shows no requests at all since {start:%H:%M} — its format "
                            "may have changed; cannot tell whether a prompt is waiting")
    waiting = prompt_waiting(open_reqs, now)
    if waiting:
        return name, False, (f"{len(waiting)} permission prompt(s) waiting on {where}'s screen: "
                             + "; ".join(waiting) + " — answer it there (Screen Sharing)")
    return name, True, f"no prompt waiting ({requests} requests, all answered)"

DISPATCH = {"service": chk_service, "mount": chk_mount, "http": chk_http, "command": chk_command,
            "hash": chk_hash, "synclag": chk_synclag, "prompts": chk_prompts}

DIRECTIVES = ("solo", "away")

def load_checks(path):
    """(checks, solo, away). checks = [(type, name, arg)]; a line that cannot be read — or a directive
    naming no check — becomes a ('config', …) entry that FAILS."""
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        sys.exit(f"fleet-local-check: config not found: {path}  (copy local-checks.conf.template)")
    checks, directives = [], []
    with open(path) as f:
        for n, line in enumerate(f, 1):
            line = line.strip()
            if not line or line.startswith("#"):
                continue
            parts = [p.strip() for p in line.split("|", 2)]
            if len(parts) == 2 and parts[0] in DIRECTIVES and parts[1]:
                directives.append((n, parts[0], parts[1], line))
                continue
            if len(parts) != 3 or parts[0] not in DISPATCH or not parts[1] or not parts[2]:
                checks.append(("config", f"config line {n}", line))
                continue
            checks.append(tuple(parts))  # (type, name, arg)
    names = {c[1] for c in checks}
    solo, away = set(), set()
    for n, kind, target, line in directives:
        # A check that returns several rows is named by its config name; each of its rows matches too.
        if target not in names:
            checks.append(("config", f"config line {n}", f"{line}  ← names no check"))
            continue
        (solo if kind == "solo" else away).add(target)
    return checks, solo, away

def run_check(typ, name, arg):
    """A LIST of (name, state, detail): most checks give one result, a registry check gives one per row."""
    if typ == "config":
        return [(name, False, f"malformed or unknown check type — {arg[:80]!r}")]
    try:
        r = DISPATCH[typ](name, arg)
    except Exception as e:
        r = (name, None, f"check crashed: {e}")      # a crashed probe has not told us anything either
    return r if isinstance(r, list) else [r]

def collect(checks):
    """Run every check and flatten: (results, {result name: the config name it came from})."""
    results, origin = [], {}
    for typ, name, arg in checks:
        for r in run_check(typ, name, arg):
            results.append(r)
            origin[r[0]] = name
    return results, origin

def load_state():
    """(states, unknown_streaks); tolerates the old flat {name: bool} format."""
    try:
        with open(STATE) as f:
            raw = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}, {}
    if raw and all(isinstance(v, bool) for v in raw.values()):
        return dict(raw), {}
    return dict(raw.get("states", {})), dict(raw.get("unknown_streaks", {}))

def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument("--config", default=os.environ.get("FLEET_LOCAL_CHECKS", DEFAULT_CONFIG))
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--always", action="store_true")
    a = ap.parse_args()
    global DRY_RUN
    DRY_RUN = a.dry_run

    checks, solo_checks, away_checks = load_checks(a.config)
    results, origin = collect(checks)
    solo = {r[0] for r in results if origin[r[0]] in solo_checks}     # a check's rows inherit its directives
    away = {r[0] for r in results if origin[r[0]] in away_checks}
    prev, streak = load_state()

    # Resolve UNKNOWN: carry the previous state forward (no transition, no mail) — but count it.
    resolved = []   # (name, ok, detail, unknown?)
    for name, ok, detail in results:
        if ok is None:
            n = streak.get(name, 0) + 1
            streak[name] = n
            carried = prev.get(name)
            carried = carried if carried is not None else True
            if name in away:                     # a target that is simply away is never escalated
                resolved.append((name, carried, f"unknown — away ({n} run{'s' if n > 1 else ''}) — {detail}", True))
            elif n >= UNKNOWN_FAIL_STREAK:
                resolved.append((name, False, f"could not determine state for {n} consecutive runs — {detail}", False))
            else:
                resolved.append((name, carried, f"unknown ({n}/{UNKNOWN_FAIL_STREAK}) — {detail}", True))
        else:
            streak.pop(name, None)
            resolved.append((name, ok, detail, False))

    cur = {r[0]: r[1] for r in resolved}
    transitions = [n for n in cur if prev.get(n) is not None and prev[n] != cur[n]]
    new_failures = [n for n in cur if not cur[n] and prev.get(n, True)]
    born_failing = [n for n in new_failures if prev.get(n) is None]   # a new check, a first run, a lost state
    failing = [r[0] for r in resolved if not r[1]]
    unknowns = [r[0] for r in resolved if r[3]]

    tag = lambda r: "????" if r[3] else ("OK  " if r[1] else "FAIL")
    report = "\n".join(f"  [{tag(r)}] {r[0]}: {r[2]}" for r in resolved)
    if a.dry_run:
        print(report or "  (no checks configured)")
        print(f"\ntransitions since last run: {transitions or 'none'}")
        if unknowns:
            print(f"unknown this run (previous state kept, no mail): {unknowns}")
        return 1 if failing else 0

    # Mail first; save state last. A check whose email did not go out keeps its PREVIOUS state, so the
    # next run sees the same change and sends it again — per check, so one failed email does not make
    # every other check repeat its alert.
    unsent = set()
    def send(kind, text, body, covers):
        # fleet-mail builds the subject — never a hand-made one here (guide § 17, "Alerts a human will actually read")
        p = subprocess.run([MAILER, "--kind", kind, "--source", "Health", "--text", text, "--body", body])
        if p.returncode != 0:
            sys.stderr.write(f"fleet-local-check: email FAILED (mailer exit {p.returncode}): {text[:70]} — "
                             "those checks keep their previous state, so the next run retries\n")
            unsent.update(covers)

    # A `solo` row mails on its own, on a transition or when it fails on its first run.
    for name in [n for n in cur if n in solo and (n in transitions or n in born_failing)]:
        r = next(x for x in resolved if x[0] == name)
        kind, text = ("Report", f"{name} recovered") if r[1] else ("Alert", f"{name}: {r[2][:70]}")
        send(kind, text, f"{name} on {os.uname().nodename}:\n\n  [{tag(r)}] {name}: {r[2]}\n\n"
                         "This check mails on its own; it is also listed in the combined health digest.\n", [name])

    # The combined digest. Solo rows stay in its BODY (a complete picture) but do not TRIGGER it, so
    # one event sends one mail. Mail on a transition AND on a failure with no previous state (a new
    # check, a first run, a lost state file) — otherwise a check that starts red never says so.
    d_trans = [n for n in transitions if n not in solo]
    d_new = [n for n in new_failures if n not in solo]
    d_born = [n for n in born_failing if n not in solo]
    if d_trans or d_new or a.always:
        # The subject is cut short, so name what is NEW first. Listed in config order, a new failure
        # behind older ones was cut off — invisible to anyone triaging by subject, which is what an
        # alert is proven by (guide § 17, "Prove the alert path").
        named = [n for n in failing if n in new_failures] + [n for n in failing if n not in new_failures]
        if d_new:
            kind, text = "Alert", f"{len(failing)} failing — {', '.join(named)[:60]}"
        elif failing:
            kind, text = "Alert", f"{len(failing)} still failing — {', '.join(named)[:60]}"
        else:
            kind, text = "Report", "all checks recovered"
        body = f"Local health checks on {os.uname().nodename}:\n\n{report}\n"
        if d_trans:
            body += f"\nChanged this run: {', '.join(d_trans)}\n"
        if d_born:
            body += f"\nNew this run, failing from its first check: {', '.join(d_born)}\n"
        if unknowns:
            body += f"\nCould not be determined this run (previous state kept, not an alert): {', '.join(unknowns)}\n"
        send(kind, text, body, d_trans + d_new)

    # Only now — after the mail went out — record what was seen. A check whose email failed keeps its
    # previous state (or none, if it had none), so the same change is seen and sent again next run.
    saved = {n: v for n, v in cur.items() if n not in unsent}
    saved.update({n: prev[n] for n in unsent if n in prev})
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(STATE), exist_ok=True)
    with open(STATE, "w") as f:
        json.dump({"states": saved, "unknown_streaks": streak}, f)
    if unsent:
        return 3
    return 1 if failing else 0

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
